            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The Holy Spirit, the breath of God, or the invisible, life-giving, and sovereignly directed power of God.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"John 3:8\",\"Acts 2:2\",\"Ezekiel 37:9\",\"Genesis 1:2\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Emptiness, futility, transience, or the brevity of human life.\",\"basis\":[\"Ecclesiastes 1:14\",\"Job 7:7\",\"Psalm 1:4\",\"Psalm 78:39\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Divine judgment, destructive forces, or severe trials (often specifically represented by the scorching East Wind).\",\"basis\":[\"Exodus 10:13\",\"Hosea 13:15\",\"Jeremiah 4:11-12\",\"Matthew 7:25\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"False teaching, instability, or shifting and deceitful doctrines.\",\"basis\":[\"Ephesians 4:14\",\"Jude 1:12\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"In both Hebrew (ruach) and Greek (pneuma), the word for wind is identical to the word for spirit and breath. This linguistic overlap makes the symbolic connection to the Holy Spirit and the breath of God inherent in the original biblical texts.\"}",
